        ":question/id": "631f3d25-1539-5b45-a5e3-359eb72f3729",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "The supplied rate function is $h'(x)=\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{x}}$ thousand dollars per week, for $x>0$.\n\nWhat was the exact rate of change of the stock's value at week $144?$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ thousand dollars per week.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The function ${h}^{'}(x)$ models the rate of change of the value of the stock, applicable in the domain of $h(x).$\n\nThus, the rate of change of the value of the stock at $x=144$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(144) & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the stock price was $\\frac{1}{60}$ thousand dollars per week.",
