        ":question/id": "4064584b-a92b-5c2b-aee4-39b559d5a1a6",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "The rate $F$ at which a swimming pool is being filled can be modeled as $F(t) = 4t^{2} - 3t$ liters per minute, where $t$ is the time, in minutes, since the pool began to be filled. The rate at which water is leaking from the pool can be modeled as $L(t) = \\frac{1}{4}t^{2}$ liters per minute, where $t$ is also the time, in minutes, since the pool began to be filled.\n\nWhat is the rate of change of the volume of water in the pool $8$ minutes after the pool began to be filled?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The rate of change of the volume of water is the filling rate minus the leaking rate.\n\n$$\nF(t)-L(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=8$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(8)-L(8) & =4(8)^{2}-3(8)-\\frac{1}{4}(8)^{2} \\\\  & =232-16 \\\\  & =216.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $216$ liters per minute.",
